          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Post-War Years and Cold War</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Churchill lost the 1945 general election but was re-elected as Prime Minister in 1951. During the Cold War, he promoted European unity and foresaw the division of Europe, coining the term \"Iron Curtain.\" His second term focused on international diplomacy and managing Britain’s waning power on the global stage.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Literature and Nobel Prize</strong><br>\nChurchill was not only a politician but also an accomplished writer. He wrote extensively about history and his experiences, earning him the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1953 for his six-volume work, \"The Second World War.\" His writings provided a deep insight into the 20th-century history and his role in it.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nChurchill’s health began to decline in the 1950s, and he retired from political life in 1955. He continued to write and paint, another of his passions, until his death on January 24, 1965. Today, he is remembered as one of the greatest leaders in British history, not only for his political career but also for his contributions to literature and the arts.</p>\n"
              },
